UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 27, Lesson 1: Comparing two viewpoints', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Comparing two viewpoints means stating each side accurately with evidence before saying which is stronger or how they differ.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWhat must you do before saying View A is better?\n\nFEEDBACK\nState View B fairly with its evidence; then compare.\n\nCHECK\nStraw-manning the other side (making it sound silly) is unfair comparison.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCompare two short viewpoint paragraphs: summarise each, list two differences and judge which uses stronger evidence.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Comparing two viewpoints means stating each side accurately with evidence before saying which is stronger or how they differ.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Reading","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-RECOM-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Compare two short viewpoint paragraphs: summarise each, list two differences and judge which uses stronger evidence. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using viewpoint on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Straw-manning the other side (making it sound silly) is unfair comparison.. Correction: State View B fairly with its evidence;. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Straw-manning the other side (making it sound silly) is unfair comparison. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating viewpoint on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with viewpoint, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about viewpoint with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for viewpoint. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for viewpoint. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","State View B fairly with its evidence","Straw-manning the other side (making it sound silly) is unfair comparison","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material"],"question":"What must you do before saying View A is better?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for viewpoint; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of compare and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Straw-manning the other side (making it sound silly) is unfair comparison","Comparing two viewpoints means stating each side accurately with evidence before saying which is stronger or how they","State View B fairly with its evidence"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with viewpoint?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named compare and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply viewpoint to new material and name the compare and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Straw-manning the other side (making it sound silly) is unfair comparison","Name compare and explain why it matters only, without linking it to viewpoint"],"question":"Which response best applies viewpoint with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries viewpoint. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event","Say the writing is good without naming any words","Straw-manning the other side (making it sound silly) is unfair comparison","Point to exact words in the sentence that show viewpoint and say what they do"],"question":"Read: \"In the dusty shed, Lila oiled the bike chain before the race.\" Which choice best uses viewpoint from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill with textual evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["State View B fairly with its evidence","Straw-manning the other side (making it sound silly) is unfair comparison","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Name compare and explain why it matters without linking it to any words in the text"],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about viewpoint?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Lengthen the answer with adjectives while keeping the same idea"],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to analyse compare and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea"],"question":"Which revision actually improves meaning for viewpoint?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accurate transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked model as the final answer","Change fonts and claim the work is new","Straw-manning the other side (making it sound silly) is unfair comparison","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about viewpoint"],"question":"Which response shows accurate use of viewpoint on fresh material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about viewpoint with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating viewpoint on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using viewpoint on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for viewpoint. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with viewpoint, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Straw-manning the other side (making it sound silly) is unfair comparison.. Correction: State View B fairly with its evidence;. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y4-w27-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise viewpoint.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use viewpoint with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify compare and explain why it matters.","I can practise evidence independently on a new prompt.","I can notice fair needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Comparing two viewpoints means stating each side accurately with evidence before saying which is stronger or how they differ.","options":["Comparing two viewpoints means stating each side accurately with evidence before saying which is stronger or how they","A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about viewpoint with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nView A: later start helps sleep. View B: earlier start matches parent work. I quote each source before judging fairness.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWhat must you do before saying View A is better?\n\nFEEDBACK\nState View B fairly with its evidence; then compare.","explain":"Comparing two viewpoints means stating each side accurately with evidence before saying which is stronger or how they differ.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Straw-manning the other side (making it sound silly) is unfair comparison. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Two viewpoints stated fairly.","caption":"Quote each before judging.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">View A</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">later start / sleep</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">View B</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">earlier / parent work</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: View A: later start helps sleep. View B: earlier start matches parent work. I quote each source before judging fairness. Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: View A: later start helps sleep. View B: earlier start matches parent work. I quote each source before judging fairness. Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation","Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about viewpoint with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nView A: later start helps sleep. View B: earlier start matches parent work. I quote each source before judging fairness. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Fair comparison vs straw-manning.","caption":"Summarise both accurately.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Fair compare</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">stronger evidence?</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Unfair</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">straw-man other side</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about viewpoint with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nStraw-manning the other side (making it sound silly) is unfair comparison. Here is a more productive model: View A: later start helps sleep. View B: earlier start matches parent work. I quote each source before judging fairness.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nState View B fairly with its evidence; then compare.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words 